WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.400
Fieldnote four. The shape of the fork.

2
00:00:09.268 --> 00:00:13.518
Who owns that provider code, and how
does it survive upstream deleting it?

3
00:00:14.958 --> 00:00:17.368
Upstream does not carry
the Azure provider.

4
00:00:17.438 --> 00:00:22.718
The fork does. A service fork is a
short list of paths the fork owns,

5
00:00:22.718 --> 00:00:25.768
beside a tree regenerated
from upstream every day.

6
00:00:27.618 --> 00:00:31.298
The fork owns the Azure
provider, its Azure tests,

7
00:00:31.298 --> 00:00:35.308
its descriptor, and the engineering
files the template delivers.

8
00:00:35.938 --> 00:00:39.228
Everything else is
upstream's, regenerated daily.

9
00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:45.458
Partition is the reference fork, and every
OSDU service the stack runs has a fork of

10
00:00:45.458 --> 00:00:46.348
this shape.

11
00:00:48.814 --> 00:00:51.774
The fork has three
branches, with three jobs.

12
00:00:51.774 --> 00:00:54.174
fork_upstream is generated input.

13
00:00:54.174 --> 00:00:57.064
fork_integration is the workspace.

14
00:00:57.214 --> 00:01:03.144
main is the protected result, and its
image is the one the stack runs by default.

15
00:01:03.294 --> 00:01:06.584
And upstream is outside all three.

16
00:01:08.956 --> 00:01:13.556
fork_upstream is generated with the
Azure paths absent by construction.

17
00:01:13.786 --> 00:01:17.556
So an upstream deletion has
nothing to delete on the fork side.

18
00:01:18.226 --> 00:01:22.986
When upstream removed its Azure provider,
fork_upstream changed only a few shared

19
00:01:22.986 --> 00:01:25.716
files, and nothing the fork owns.

20
00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:28.436
Main still holds the provider.

21
00:01:31.118 --> 00:01:34.968
Follow a fix like the cache
fallback, made in the fork today.

22
00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:37.608
From here the journey is illustrative.

23
00:01:37.838 --> 00:01:40.638
The fix lands in the fork-owned directory,

24
00:01:40.638 --> 00:01:44.648
with its tests. The workflows
the template delivered check it.

25
00:01:44.958 --> 00:01:46.808
It reaches main, approved.

26
00:01:47.118 --> 00:01:49.368
Meanwhile, upstream moves:

27
00:01:49.518 --> 00:01:52.158
a change to partition-core.

28
00:01:52.158 --> 00:01:57.688
And tonight at midnight UTC,
fork_upstream is regenerated.

29
00:01:58.158 --> 00:02:01.048
Two changes are now waiting to meet.

30
00:02:03.258 --> 00:02:04.508
An easy mistake:

31
00:02:04.578 --> 00:02:08.508
a fork is a copy we took
once, and patch as we go.

32
00:02:09.058 --> 00:02:11.868
The service fork keeps a
relationship with upstream,

33
00:02:12.018 --> 00:02:13.308
not a copy of it.

34
00:02:13.938 --> 00:02:18.268
Every day the filter regenerates
fork_upstream from the upstream tip.

35
00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:22.028
To check, read the
generated commit's trailers.

36
00:02:24.886 --> 00:02:26.126
So now you can say:

37
00:02:26.126 --> 00:02:31.576
the fork owns a short list of paths, and
everything else is regenerated daily.

38
00:02:31.726 --> 00:02:35.976
Three branches, three jobs, with
upstream outside all three.

39
00:02:36.766 --> 00:02:42.366
And fork_upstream carries no Azure paths,
so an upstream deletion has nothing to

40
00:02:42.366 --> 00:02:43.096
delete.

41
00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.310
Next: what happens to a provider
change, and to upstream's change,

42
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.200
between midnight and an image digest?

